{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790959613,"nonce":"tISHXb7S3YfaQVYRkRHgLmNQYwM=","title":"a HashLink Buffer is read as a String: haxe.io.Bytes has its length first","message":"HashLink lays out `String` as `{type, bytes, length}` and `haxe.io.Bytes` as `{type, length, b}` (std/hl/_std: `String` declares `bytes` then `length`; `haxe.io.Bytes` declares `length` then `b`).\n\nThe HashLink target treats every `Buffer` and `BufferMut` as the first:\n\n- an argument is signed `OBi_` and arrives as `*mut hl_abi::vstring`, though the Haxe surface passes a `haxe.io.Bytes`;\n- `XidlBytes.copy(value, out:haxe.io.Bytes)` is served by an adapter primitive that hlwgpu writes over `*mut vstring` too.\n\nSo the native code reads the length as the data pointer's low half and the data pointer as the length. Stock HashLink would refuse the `OBi_` signature for a `haxe.io.Bytes` argument; Ash does not check signatures, so the call goes through and reads, or writes, the wrong memory.\n\nReproducer, on Ash with xwindow's HashLink surface: a native returning `Buffer` [47, 255] through `XidlBytes.take` gives Haxe `[0, 0]`. The copy wrote its two bytes to an address made of the Bytes' length and padding.\n\nFix: a `Buffer` argument is signed `OiB_` and arrives as a generated `HlBytes { t, length: i32, b }`, which `Buffer::from_hl` takes; the generated surface defines `buffer_result_len` and `buffer_result_copy` over the same layout, with the carrier's `len` and `as_ptr`. Adapters stop defining those two primitives and take `*mut HlBytes` in `Buffer::from_hl` and `BufferMut::from_hl`. hlwgpu's runtime needs both changes when it moves to this revision; its buffer uploads are affected today.","files":null}]}